WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.669
- The one issue, for example,
where I depart pretty strongly

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:04.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the traditional
Republican line

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.873
is I think a lot of folks
in the Republican Party,

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:09.442 align:start size:97% position:3%
they see what's going on
with Google, Apple, Facebook,

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:11.778
with big tech writ
large and they say,

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:14.214
well, we don't like
what they're doing,

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:16.116
we don't like the fact that
they sensor conservatives,

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:17.484
but they're private companies

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:19.719
and so we can't actually
do anything with them.

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:21.421
And my response to that
is, well, first of all,

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.889
they're not actually
private companies,

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:25.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
they benefit from a ton of
special government privileges,

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:27.327
but also they're
just too powerful.

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:30.397 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like however they got there,
they're way too powerful

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:33.566 align:start position:0%
and they're way too big at this
point, so I'm willing to go

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:36.036 align:start position:0%
with Ken Buck and with Josh
Hawley and with others and say,

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.271
look, we've gotta
actually reign in

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.573 align:start size:97% position:3%
the power of these companies
because if they control

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:42.175
what you're allowed
to say in America,

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.610
we don't have a First
Amendment anymore.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.577
If we don't have
a First Amendment,

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.146
we don't have a real country.

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:48.715
(gentle music)

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:54.687 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.990 line:0%
is the author of
"Hillbilly Elegy:

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.193 line:0%
A Memoir of a Family
and Culture in Crisis,"

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
founder of the venture
capital firm Narya,

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:07.000
and current Senate candidate
from the great state of Ohio.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.135 align:start size:84% position:16%
J.D. Vance, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.570
- Thanks for having me,
Dave, good to see you.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.105
- Good to be with ya.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:14.107
You know, I've wanted to
chat with you for a while

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.209
because I find that on Twitter,

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.812
I find some people
who make some sense,

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:22.115 align:start size:97% position:3%
who actually are saying some
things that actually seem

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.617 align:start size:94% position:6%
like a sensible way
forward for this country,

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:28.188 align:start position:0%
for this earth, the whole thing,
you're on that short list.

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.323 align:start size:84% position:16%
For people that don't
know who you are,

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.858
can you give a little
bit of the backstory,

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:33.560
that's sort of what the book
is about and then the movie,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.428
but can you just give
me a little brief bio

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.596
just to kick us off?

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:38.932 line:0%
- Yeah, sure.

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:41.267 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
So I was raised by my
working-class grandparents

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.470 line:0%
in Middletown, Ohio, which
is in southwestern Ohio,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.005
it's a steel town,
and the basic story

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:48.641
is because of a host
of complicated reasons,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.143 align:start size:88% position:13%
but one of which is
that my mother struggled

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:51.644
with opioid addiction,

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.780
you know, my grandparents
had to take care of me

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.615
and we grew up in a pretty
rough and tumble environment,

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:58.318
didn't have a lot of money,
didn't have a lot of support,

00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:59.686
but things sort of
worked out for me.

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
As I like to say,
America worked for me,

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.357
and I think it really did
for a number of reasons,

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.593
but two reasons at
the top of the list

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:10.396
are my grandma was always
very loving and supportive.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.699
She was, as you know, Dave,
'cause you read the book,

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:15.034
but she was not a traditional
grandmother in a lot of ways.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.202
She loved to say the F word,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
she had 19 handguns
when she died,

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:22.041
but she was also a woman
of incredibly deep faith.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:24.777 align:start position:0%
And then I also was well-served
by the U.S. Marine Corps

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.880
and I enlisted in April of 2003,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.649 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is right as the
Iraq War was ramping up.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:31.718
You know, Marine Corps is this
incredible experience for me,

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.887
gave me a lot of
important lessons,

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:35.688
a lot of lifelong friendships,

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:40.693
and then from there, went to
college, went to law school,

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:42.729 align:start size:88% position:13%
sort of started working
in Silicon Valley

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:44.731
in the technology industry,

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:47.400 align:start size:84% position:16%
got connected with
Peter Thiel out there,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then while I was
working with Peter actually,

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.304
published this book,
"Hillbilly Elegy,"

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.940
which happened to coincide,
how could I possibly known

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.642
with this remarkable
political environment

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:59.412 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we lived in in 2016 where
Donald Trump on the right,

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.647
was coming out of nowhere

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:02.382
and taking over the
Republican Party,

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:03.917 align:start size:84% position:16%
Bernie Sanders was
coming out of the left

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:06.286 align:start size:84% position:16%
and almost taking over
the Democrat Party,

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.288
and so I sort of found
myself all of a sudden

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:09.489
a national commentator,

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:11.591 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm glad that
you liked my Twitter.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.994
You know, a lot of
your colleagues in
the media don't, but.

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:17.230
(J.D. and Dave laughing)

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm not sure
they're my colleagues,

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.901 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they are in the
media, whatever that is.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.703 align:start size:88% position:13%
But you bring up an
interesting point there

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:27.040 align:start size:97% position:3%
because yeah, that sort of put
you on the map around 2016.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.142 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Trump thing's
happening and in many ways,

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:31.945 align:start size:88% position:13%
your story and the book
and then the movie,

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:34.414 align:start size:84% position:16%
sort of is the
story of the people

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:37.116 align:start size:84% position:16%
that kind of supported
Trump, right?

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.651
- Yeah, it is.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.386
So you think of the story of
my family, so my grandfather

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
was like a working-class,
Blue Dog Democrat.

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:45.525 align:start size:84% position:16%
He was a classic,
steel union, Democrat.

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:49.996 align:start size:97% position:3%
Socially moderate, very
patriotic, loved his country,

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:51.531
but sort of always saw Democrats

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:54.234
as the party of the working
man and the working women.

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:58.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
By the time Trump
came around in 2016,

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
most of those
non-college educated

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:03.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
classic white
working-class white voters,

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.412
that was sort of the
buzz word in 2016,

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:07.647
they had shifted
so hard to Trump.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.716
And so I think a
lot of the reason

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:12.585
that the book did very well,
is people were picking it up,

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.488
trying to understand like
who are all of these voters

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:18.725
who even, you know, a lot of
white working-class voters

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:22.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
actually supported a Barack
Obama in 2008 or 2012,

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.197
but had this really
hard shift right

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:25.698
in support of Donald Trump,

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so I think a lot of folk
were trying to understand

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:28.901
what was going on,

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.870
and the book obviously
benefited from that.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.739
- Were you surprised
that there was a jump

00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:37.176
from those values being in
the middle of the country,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
steelworkers, industry's
changing, all that,

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.981
and then to an actual
political movement

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.751 align:start size:97% position:3%
from a guy who was a real
estate developer in New York?

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:47.287
- You know, it's interesting.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:51.157
I definitely found the
reaction to the book

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:53.726 align:start size:97% position:3%
as sort of this political
insight into a lot of voters,

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:56.829
a little bit weird 'cause the
book doesn't mention Clinton,

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.866 align:start size:97% position:3%
it doesn't mention Trump, it's
not really a political book

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:02.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the classic sense,
but I understand

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:03.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
why people were
trying to understand,

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.538
like why did these folks in
the middle of the country

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:08.775 align:start position:0%
feel left behind, so
condescended to, and of course,

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:10.543
they feel left behind,
they feel condescended to

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:12.545 align:start size:84% position:16%
because in many cases,
they actually are.

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:14.013
They have been left behind,

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:17.417 align:start position:0%
they have been looked down upon
by a lot of folks in power,

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:20.553 align:start position:0%
so I appreciate that people
were at least for a brief period

00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:23.489
trying to understand that
demographic of the country.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.658 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, obviously the book
benefited from that

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.694
and I think hopefully it
did provide some insights

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:30.830
from that group of people
or to that group of people.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.799
- Yeah, you mentioned
the Blue Dog Democrat.

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:34.467 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm not sure how much
you know about my story,

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:37.103
but I was a lefty, I was a
progressive, the whole thing,

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:40.740
but I would say if the Blue
Dog Democrats still existed,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.042
but they're basically like
the unicorn at this point,

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:46.045
there would be maybe
some room for me to say

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:49.148 align:start size:97% position:3%
I could kinda be on that side,
probably not at this point,

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:51.250
but do you think that
person exists anymore

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.919
and if they do exist,

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:55.088
are they basically just
a modern conservative?

00:05:55.088 --> 00:05:58.291 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, is that sort of
what you are at this point?

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:01.060 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, I think that the people
like that certainly exist,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.629 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think Donald Trump fits into
that, where to your point,

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.264
even though he was a
real estate developer,

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:08.968
I think he's at his cart,
he's a guy from Queens

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:11.104
who works with a lot of
working-class people,

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:13.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
construction workers,
salespeople, and so forth,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so he's always
identified with those folks

00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:18.578
at a deep emotional level and
they've identified with him.

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:20.012
You know, Donald
Trump is up there,

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:23.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
I certainly think my
attitude towards politics

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:26.386
is like a working class or
a blue collar conservatism.

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:28.388
There were other people out
there, I think Jim Banks

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:30.523
is a really interesting guy,
a representative from Indiana,

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:32.959
I think Tucker Carlson,
talk about another guy

00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:37.563
who's sort of an East Coast,
WASP in the classic sense,

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:40.700
but I think identifies
with blue collar folks

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.968
in a very deep way

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:43.703
and obviously focuses a
lot on them in his show,

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.105 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I think there are actually
a lot of us out there

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:49.075
who think the purpose
of the Republican Party

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.910
should be to better
serve its voters,

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:54.547
but I think they're all on
the right at this point.

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:57.316
I mean, there were maybe a
couple of Blue Dog Democrats,

00:06:57.316 --> 00:07:00.019 align:start size:88% position:13%
even five, 10 years ago,
they all got primary,

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:02.355
they all got effectively
kicked out of the party,

00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:04.223
and they're just clearly
not welcome there anymore,

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:05.458
so if they exist at all,

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:07.693
they're sort of our
side of the aisle.

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:08.895
- Yeah, I mean, that's
what I've been saying.

00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:10.797
I know a few of these people

00:07:10.797 --> 00:07:13.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think it's kind
of a worthy fight

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:15.268
if you wanna still
say you're a Democrat

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:18.237
and try to do something as
the house is burning down,

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.473
but it just doesn't seem
like it could work to me.

00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:24.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you surprised how
radical the left has gone,

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:25.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
that there is no room
for these people?

00:07:25.845 --> 00:07:27.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I guess as someone
running for Senate now,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.348
you're kind of excited.

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:30.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, yeah, come over
here, we're all right.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.485
(J.D. chuckles)

00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:36.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I'm definitely surprised
and a little startled

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:38.691 align:start size:97% position:3%
by how crazy the left has
gone over the last 10 years.

00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:39.926
I mean, in part,

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:41.928
this is a consequence
of demographics, right?

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:44.397
The left is basically the party

00:07:44.397 --> 00:07:48.134
of upper middle-class
and upper-class elites,

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:49.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then some
working-class minorities

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:51.304
who are sort of still
along for the ride

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:53.406
on the Democratic Party,
but really those folks

00:07:53.406 --> 00:07:56.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
don't have a ton of influence
in the party, right?

00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:59.345
So it's partially just sort
of who is leading the left.

00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:02.548
I think it's like, you know,
professionally educated people

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:04.250
who went to Ivy league schools

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.819 align:start size:97% position:3%
who tend to have pretty
radical views about politics

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.054
because of it.

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:10.122
I think a little of it
is definitely economic,

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:13.493 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that the left is not
comfortable with the fact

00:08:13.493 --> 00:08:15.294 align:start size:84% position:16%
that a lot of people
are dissatisfied

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:17.563
with the way the
economy has worked,

00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:19.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
the way the manufacturing
economy has worked,

00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:21.067
and instead of saying,

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:22.435
you know, maybe this
is a serious problem

00:08:22.435 --> 00:08:24.570 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we've like lost
our entire manufacturing

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.072
and industrial base to China,

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:28.541
I think their response is
to complain that the people

00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:30.443 align:start size:97% position:3%
who care about our industrial
base in the first place,

00:08:30.443 --> 00:08:33.045
they're obviously racist
or xenophobic radiates.

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:34.580 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I think part
of what's going on

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.849
is it's just like the
composition of the left

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:38.651
has really radically changed,

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:39.886
but then part of what's going on

00:08:39.886 --> 00:08:43.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
is the left, completely
unable to solve

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.424
America's real problems
and sort of resorted

00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:47.894 align:start size:84% position:16%
to criticizing most
Americans as backwards

00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:51.130
or racist in some way, and so,
yeah, it's really startling,

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.300
it's really depressing, but
you know, it is where we is,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.136 align:start position:0%
and you know, it is what it
is, and I think, unfortunately,

00:08:57.136 --> 00:08:59.372 align:start size:84% position:16%
we just have to
fight these people.

00:08:59.372 --> 00:09:01.674
- Where do you think Trump
now fits into this thing?

00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:04.710
Because you were not onboard
the Trump train in 2016, right?

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.580
You did vote for him in 2020.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.916
Where do you think
he sort of is now,

00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:12.485 align:start size:84% position:16%
like the worth of
Trump at this point?

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:15.922
- Oh yeah, you're right, I
was a late adopter to Trump

00:09:15.922 --> 00:09:17.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I remember Peter Thiel
giving me crap for it

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:19.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
back in 2016, and of
course, eventually I said,

00:09:19.992 --> 00:09:21.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, you're
right, you we're right.

00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:26.399
The guy was saying a
lot of smart things,

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:28.367
did support him in 2020.

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:29.902
I mean, I think at
a fundamental level,

00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:33.372
Trump is still the leader of
the Republican Party, right?

00:09:33.372 --> 00:09:34.874
So to the extent that you have

00:09:34.874 --> 00:09:37.677
this blue collar patriotic
movement within the country,

00:09:37.677 --> 00:09:39.178
it's on the right,

00:09:39.178 --> 00:09:41.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
most of those voters are still
very loyal to Donald Trump,

00:09:41.814 --> 00:09:45.084 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so I think he continues
to have a very important voice

00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:46.619
either as a king
maker in the party,

00:09:46.619 --> 00:09:48.454
certainly I think
if he ran in 2024

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:52.058
there's a very good chance he'd
get elected president again,

00:09:52.058 --> 00:09:56.128 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I tend to think that
Trump is not gonna disappear

00:09:56.128 --> 00:09:58.264 align:start size:84% position:16%
as a force from
American public life.

00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:01.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think, you
know, unfortunately,

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.937 align:start size:97% position:3%
most Republicans or at least
most Republican leaders,

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:07.640 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think most Republican voters
are very good about this,

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.075
most Republican leaders

00:10:09.075 --> 00:10:11.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think don't fully appreciate
what Trump represented,

00:10:11.911 --> 00:10:15.748 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so there isn't an
obvious like answer to Trump

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:17.049
if he just disappeared tomorrow.

00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:18.618
I do fear a little bit

00:10:18.618 --> 00:10:20.820
that if Trump just went
the way of the Dodo,

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.722
you know, decided, well, I'm
gonna focus on making money

00:10:22.722 --> 00:10:23.923
or I'm gonna go
play golf all day

00:10:23.923 --> 00:10:25.858
and not really worry
about politics anymore,

00:10:25.858 --> 00:10:27.760
like, I don't know that
there's an obvious person

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:28.995
to replace him, right?

00:10:28.995 --> 00:10:30.563 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's some really good
people like DeSantis,

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:31.764
like Josh Hawley,

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:36.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
but outside of a few
really bright politicians,

00:10:36.168 --> 00:10:38.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think most people
don't wanna fully embrace

00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:40.239
what Trump represented
for the Republican Party,

00:10:40.239 --> 00:10:43.209
which is a tragedy, 'cause
I think it's ultimately,

00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:45.511
you know, equivocal with
some things on the margins,

00:10:45.511 --> 00:10:48.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I think it's the right
direction for the party to go.

00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:50.416
- Right, so you're saying guys
like the DeSantis and Hawley

00:10:50.416 --> 00:10:52.618 align:start size:84% position:16%
are better than
Romney and Liz Cheney,

00:10:52.618 --> 00:10:55.955
is that the controversial
statement you're making?

00:10:55.955 --> 00:10:57.923 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, the radical
claim that I would make

00:10:57.923 --> 00:11:00.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the Josh Hawley is much
better than Liz Cheney

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:03.262 align:start size:88% position:13%
and Ron DeSantis is
better than Mitt Romney.

00:11:03.262 --> 00:11:05.598 align:start size:88% position:13%
But again, unfortunately
I actually think

00:11:05.598 --> 00:11:09.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
the most elected Republicans
are not as far out there

00:11:09.869 --> 00:11:11.804
as Liz Cheney.

00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:15.041
They really don't like
what Trump represents.

00:11:15.041 --> 00:11:17.076
They sort of want the
party to be what it was

00:11:17.076 --> 00:11:20.012
during the Bush era, 2000, 2004,

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:22.148
and because of that, there's
still a lot of resistance.

00:11:22.148 --> 00:11:23.716
I mean, even one of
the big takeaways

00:11:23.716 --> 00:11:25.251
from the Trump administration

00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:28.821 line:0%
is that when the entire party
is institutionally set against

00:11:28.821 --> 00:11:30.756 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
the will of the
president himself,

00:11:30.756 --> 00:11:33.292 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
there can be a lot of
real bureaucratic fights,

00:11:33.292 --> 00:11:35.127 line:0%
and I think so much
of what happened

00:11:35.127 --> 00:11:36.662
during the Trump administration

00:11:36.662 --> 00:11:39.632
is the Trump folks wanted
to do something on trade,

00:11:39.632 --> 00:11:41.133
they were somewhat successful,

00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:43.369
they were also fought by
members of their own party,

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:45.504
and so you don't just
need one politician,

00:11:45.504 --> 00:11:48.741
you sort of need the entire
institution to reform itself.

00:11:48.741 --> 00:11:50.710
- Right, so where do
you fit into that,

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.279
like in terms of your
beliefs at this point?

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are they all traditionally
conservative?

00:11:56.716 --> 00:11:58.184 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, I get what
you're saying,

00:11:58.184 --> 00:12:00.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
you could maybe be thought
of as one of those,

00:12:00.386 --> 00:12:03.589 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, 15-year-ago
Blue Dog Democrat types,

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:04.824
but like what would say

00:12:04.824 --> 00:12:08.494
your broad sort of
idea set look like?

00:12:08.494 --> 00:12:09.862
- Yeah, I mean,

00:12:09.862 --> 00:12:11.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are some traditionally
conservative ideas

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.099
that I'm very comfortable with.

00:12:13.099 --> 00:12:14.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm very pro-life,
I've always been pro-life,

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:16.435
I've always cared a
lot about that issue

00:12:16.435 --> 00:12:19.538
because I think when abortion
becomes widespread in society,

00:12:19.538 --> 00:12:22.374
what it effectively does is
send a message to families

00:12:22.374 --> 00:12:24.276
that their babies
are inconveniences

00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:27.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
instead of real like
blessings for us to support.

00:12:27.213 --> 00:12:30.216
So on some issues I'm very
traditionally conservative,

00:12:30.216 --> 00:12:31.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, but like one
issue, for example,

00:12:31.717 --> 00:12:33.018
where I depart pretty strongly

00:12:33.018 --> 00:12:35.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the traditional
Republican line

00:12:35.054 --> 00:12:37.456
is I think a lot of folks
in the Republican Party,

00:12:37.456 --> 00:12:40.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
they see what's going on
with Google, Apple, Facebook,

00:12:40.025 --> 00:12:42.361
with big tech writ
large, and they say,

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:44.830
well, we don't like
what they're doing,

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.732
we don't like the fact that
they sensor conservatives,

00:12:46.732 --> 00:12:48.100
but they're private companies

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.369
and so we can't actually
do anything with them,

00:12:50.369 --> 00:12:51.871
and my response to that is,

00:12:51.871 --> 00:12:53.372 align:start position:0%
well, first of all, they're
not actually private companies,

00:12:53.372 --> 00:12:55.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
they benefit from a ton of
special government privileges,

00:12:55.975 --> 00:12:57.943
but also they're
just too powerful.

00:12:57.943 --> 00:13:00.913 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like however they got there,
they're way too powerful

00:13:00.913 --> 00:13:02.548
and they're way too
big at this point,

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:04.650
so I'm willing to
go with Ken Buck

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.119
and with Josh Hawley and
with others and say, look,

00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:09.922
we've gotta actually reign in
the power of these companies

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:11.157
because if they control

00:13:11.157 --> 00:13:12.658
what you're allowed
to say in America,

00:13:12.658 --> 00:13:14.093
we don't have a First
Amendment anymore.

00:13:14.093 --> 00:13:15.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
If we don't have
the First Amendment,

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.662
we don't have a real country.

00:13:16.662 --> 00:13:18.964
- Yeah, it's interesting
because I've discussed this

00:13:18.964 --> 00:13:20.332
with Tucker on his
show many times,

00:13:20.332 --> 00:13:21.967
and years, like two
or three years ago,

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:24.804 align:start position:0%
I was still like no regulation,
don't touch anything,

00:13:24.804 --> 00:13:26.038
the libertarian side of me,

00:13:26.038 --> 00:13:28.007
and I actually have
come more towards

00:13:28.007 --> 00:13:29.408
what you just laid out there,

00:13:29.408 --> 00:13:31.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is what Josh
Hawley's been laying out.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what do you think
that actually looks like?

00:13:33.245 --> 00:13:35.548
Because if the founders
could have never imagined

00:13:35.548 --> 00:13:37.383
something more powerful
than the government,

00:13:37.383 --> 00:13:38.851
which is what we've got,

00:13:38.851 --> 00:13:40.786
I mean, do you wanna send
government bureaucrats

00:13:40.786 --> 00:13:43.255 align:start position:0%
to look at the Google algorithm
as if they could do it

00:13:43.255 --> 00:13:45.691
or is it breaking
these companies up?

00:13:45.691 --> 00:13:47.326
I mean, I know you like
the competition side

00:13:47.326 --> 00:13:49.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we'll talk about Rumble
in just a little bit,

00:13:49.228 --> 00:13:51.163
but like what does
it actually look like

00:13:51.163 --> 00:13:52.998
that you would want
done to big tech?

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:55.367
- Yeah, yeah, so yeah,
bracketing the things

00:13:55.367 --> 00:13:56.669
you can still do in
the private sector,

00:13:56.669 --> 00:13:57.903
which I think is important,

00:13:57.903 --> 00:14:00.072
I think that there
are ranges of options

00:14:00.072 --> 00:14:02.541
from less radical to
more radical, right?

00:14:02.541 --> 00:14:04.476 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I think the slightly
less radical option

00:14:04.476 --> 00:14:05.945
is you just make in the same way

00:14:05.945 --> 00:14:08.681 align:start size:97% position:3%
that like you can't censor
somebody because they're black

00:14:08.681 --> 00:14:10.182
or because they're white.

00:14:10.182 --> 00:14:11.784
Well, you know, you used
to not be able to do that,

00:14:11.784 --> 00:14:13.786
of course, the last
couple of years.

00:14:13.786 --> 00:14:14.720 align:start size:84% position:16%
(J.D. laughs)
- Yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:15.988
give it a couple more months.

00:14:15.988 --> 00:14:18.591
- You know, you can't
for example, turn off,

00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:20.893
an electric utility can't
turn off somebody's power

00:14:20.893 --> 00:14:24.096
because they don't like the
sex or the gender or the race

00:14:24.096 --> 00:14:25.798
of the people who
need the power.

00:14:25.798 --> 00:14:27.766
I think one thing you
could do is just add

00:14:27.766 --> 00:14:30.603
political viewpoint to
that protected class.

00:14:30.603 --> 00:14:31.637
Say if you're a conservative

00:14:31.637 --> 00:14:33.172
and you're censored on Facebook,

00:14:33.172 --> 00:14:34.907
you basically have a
right to file a lawsuit

00:14:34.907 --> 00:14:36.375
in the same way that
if you're censored

00:14:36.375 --> 00:14:38.244 align:start size:97% position:3%
because you're black, you have
a right to file a lawsuit.

00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:39.778
That's sort of option one.

00:14:39.778 --> 00:14:42.948 align:start position:0%
I think a little bit more
radical is the antitrust option,

00:14:42.948 --> 00:14:45.851
effectively breaking
up these companies,

00:14:45.851 --> 00:14:49.121
making it impossible for
them to sort of control

00:14:49.121 --> 00:14:51.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
the discourse because
you have more competition

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.025
among the platforms
that are out there.

00:14:53.025 --> 00:14:56.161 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, I'm basically
supportive of that,

00:14:56.161 --> 00:14:57.830
though I think you have
to do it the right way

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:00.032
or effectively these sort
of companies just reform

00:15:00.032 --> 00:15:01.333
as natural monopolies.

00:15:02.501 --> 00:15:04.403
The thing that I'm
really intrigued by

00:15:04.403 --> 00:15:07.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
is like, does the business
model of Silicon Valley

00:15:07.072 --> 00:15:08.340
actually makes sense, right?

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.442
So, you know, I have
my iPhone right here,

00:15:10.442 --> 00:15:12.912 align:start size:88% position:13%
if you think about
like what an iPhone is,

00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:15.147
it really is a thing that
gets you to stare at it

00:15:15.147 --> 00:15:18.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
as much as possible so
that it can take your data

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:19.285
and then sell it back to you

00:15:19.285 --> 00:15:20.719 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the form of
targeted advertising.

00:15:20.719 --> 00:15:21.887
There's a pretty good argument

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:23.956 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the Silicon
Valley business model,

00:15:23.956 --> 00:15:26.125
which distracts a
lot of attention

00:15:26.125 --> 00:15:29.094
and effectively is built
upon the theft of data

00:15:29.094 --> 00:15:30.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
should be completely
outlawed, right?

00:15:30.963 --> 00:15:35.067
Make it illegal to sort
of collect people's data

00:15:35.067 --> 00:15:37.770
without a whole host of
additional protections.

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:40.039
I think that would blow up
the model of Silicon Valley,

00:15:40.039 --> 00:15:43.475 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think maybe
that's too radical,

00:15:43.475 --> 00:15:44.944
but if I'm actually
laying my cards out.

00:15:44.944 --> 00:15:47.146
- Hey, as a guy that just
started a tech company

00:15:47.146 --> 00:15:49.815
that's doing subscription
and doesn't sell data,

00:15:49.815 --> 00:15:51.050
keep it going, keep it going.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:54.286
- Yeah, but I had to
say that honestly,

00:15:54.286 --> 00:15:55.721
I would be supportive of that.

00:15:55.721 --> 00:15:57.089
If you basically said

00:15:57.089 --> 00:15:59.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
you can no longer effectively
steal people's data,

00:15:59.325 --> 00:16:01.460 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that would be
really good for our country

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.662
and I actually think it would
be good for the tech sector

00:16:03.662 --> 00:16:06.265
because like the tech sector,

00:16:06.265 --> 00:16:09.301 align:start size:97% position:3%
whether it's in digital
technology or pharmaceuticals

00:16:09.301 --> 00:16:11.403
or energy and transportation,

00:16:11.403 --> 00:16:15.374
like it's become a little
bit, like these devices,

00:16:15.374 --> 00:16:18.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think have become a
little parasitic on capital,

00:16:18.811 --> 00:16:21.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
on high quality talent,
and there's this weird way

00:16:21.613 --> 00:16:22.815
where our entire economy,

00:16:22.815 --> 00:16:25.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
like Silicon Valley
has worked pretty well,

00:16:25.584 --> 00:16:27.052
if you look at the S&amp;P 500,

00:16:27.052 --> 00:16:29.588
Silicon Valley has great
returns in their companies,

00:16:29.588 --> 00:16:32.124
but like the entire
rest of the country

00:16:32.124 --> 00:16:33.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the entire
rest of the economy

00:16:33.492 --> 00:16:35.060
has been relatively stagnant.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:36.295
That's a big problem.

00:16:36.295 --> 00:16:37.863
That's not just a
censorship problem,

00:16:37.863 --> 00:16:39.598
that's a broader
economic problem,

00:16:39.598 --> 00:16:41.533 align:start position:0%
but I think we have to be
worried about that problem too,

00:16:41.533 --> 00:16:43.135
so I'm pretty radical on this.

00:16:43.135 --> 00:16:44.603
In other words,

00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:46.238 align:start size:94% position:6%
go after their special
privileges, break them up,

00:16:46.238 --> 00:16:49.274
maybe even illegalize the
entire business model,

00:16:49.274 --> 00:16:51.343
because I care about
having a real economy

00:16:51.343 --> 00:16:52.845
in a real country again.

00:16:52.845 --> 00:16:54.079
- At some level though,

00:16:54.079 --> 00:16:55.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
do you think it's
just kinda too late?

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:59.218 align:start position:0%
Like we had Trump for four
years, we had all the hearings,

00:16:59.218 --> 00:17:01.687
we all saw Zuckerberg
and Jack from Twitter

00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:04.590
and the rest of them trot out
there and basically make up

00:17:04.590 --> 00:17:06.859
a lot of nonsense half
the time under oath,

00:17:06.859 --> 00:17:08.961
and we didn't do anything then,

00:17:08.961 --> 00:17:11.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
so why would the Biden
administration that obviously

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:14.500 align:start size:97% position:3%
is no fan of some of the ideas
you're putting out there,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:16.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
why would they be
the ones to do this?

00:17:16.502 --> 00:17:18.003
- Well, I don't think they're
gonna be the ones to do this.

00:17:18.003 --> 00:17:19.938
I mean, I definitely think
it's gonna be a combination

00:17:19.938 --> 00:17:22.007 align:start size:84% position:16%
of leverage from
Republican senators,

00:17:22.007 --> 00:17:24.510
and hopefully I'm one in
the not too distant future,

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.712
it's also gonna require a new
Republican administration.

00:17:26.712 --> 00:17:27.980
I think, again,
all of this stuff

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:30.049
is gonna happen on the
right because the left

00:17:30.049 --> 00:17:33.552
is way too in the pocket
of the big tech industry.

00:17:33.552 --> 00:17:35.287
All that said, you know,

00:17:35.287 --> 00:17:37.122
I try to take a very
long view in this.

00:17:37.122 --> 00:17:38.557
You may know this
guy, Nigel Farrage,

00:17:38.557 --> 00:17:41.160
he's sort of the
leader of the UPA.

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:42.995
- I've gotten drunk with him.

00:17:42.995 --> 00:17:46.398
- Okay, well, yeah, he's a
good guy to have a beer with.

00:17:47.599 --> 00:17:49.201
You know, one of the
things that I've taken

00:17:49.201 --> 00:17:51.136 align:start size:88% position:13%
just from getting to
know Nigel a little bit,

00:17:51.136 --> 00:17:54.740
is like the Brexit
thing took 25 years

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:57.843 align:start size:88% position:13%
of institution building,
of convincing voters,

00:17:57.843 --> 00:18:01.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
of convincing media, a ton
of institutional biases

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:03.382
against the Brexit movement,

00:18:03.382 --> 00:18:05.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
electorally there were
some weird issues,

00:18:05.451 --> 00:18:07.786
of course, the media
hated Nigel Farrage

00:18:07.786 --> 00:18:09.521
and thought he was
a terrible person,

00:18:09.521 --> 00:18:13.025
so I tend to think about
this in a longer-term way,

00:18:13.025 --> 00:18:15.828 align:start size:97% position:3%
that like the goal here
isn't to fix this tomorrow.

00:18:15.828 --> 00:18:18.897
Yes, you know, I wish that
more could have happened

00:18:18.897 --> 00:18:20.199
over the last four years

00:18:20.199 --> 00:18:22.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
in actually addressing
the tech problem,

00:18:22.468 --> 00:18:23.836
but I think if we
take that attitude

00:18:23.836 --> 00:18:25.070
it can become self-defeating

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:26.605
and what we have to
do in this moment

00:18:26.605 --> 00:18:28.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
is actually gear
ourselves up for a fight,

00:18:28.841 --> 00:18:30.909
in a long-term fight,
and if we do that,

00:18:30.909 --> 00:18:32.411
I actually think the
people are with us,

00:18:32.411 --> 00:18:34.813
I think that we're on the
right side of this issue

00:18:34.813 --> 00:18:36.048
and we'll eventually win,

00:18:36.048 --> 00:18:38.150
it just may take some
work and a lot of time.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:41.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, do you think we just
need a sort of separation

00:18:41.019 --> 00:18:43.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
from all of the institutions
that the left has built

00:18:43.956 --> 00:18:45.457
that we're now watching crumble?

00:18:45.457 --> 00:18:48.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, do we need to just
build new institutions,

00:18:48.327 --> 00:18:51.363 align:start size:84% position:16%
new technological
answers and solutions,

00:18:51.363 --> 00:18:54.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
just really like a
complete parallel economy?

00:18:55.834 --> 00:18:57.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I mean, I
think we probably do.

00:18:57.669 --> 00:18:59.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
We need to build parallel
businesses of course,

00:18:59.638 --> 00:19:00.839
because the businesses

00:19:00.839 --> 00:19:02.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
have gone fully in
the tank for the left,

00:19:02.741 --> 00:19:04.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
like the biggest
multinational corporations

00:19:04.743 --> 00:19:08.413 align:start size:88% position:13%
are now effectively
enterprises of the left.

00:19:08.413 --> 00:19:09.515
I also think we have to build

00:19:09.515 --> 00:19:11.383 align:start size:88% position:13%
alternative educational
institutions.

00:19:11.383 --> 00:19:12.551
You know, one of the things

00:19:12.551 --> 00:19:14.520 align:start size:88% position:13%
the left has been
incredibly effective at

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.055
is monopolizing education
in this country, right?

00:19:17.055 --> 00:19:19.258
They basically created a system

00:19:19.258 --> 00:19:21.760
whereby if you don't get a
four year college degree,

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:23.629
it's very hard to even apply

00:19:23.629 --> 00:19:25.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
for most middle-class
jobs, right?

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:28.100
Even if they don't really
require a college degree,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.501
they'll put it right
on the application,

00:19:29.501 --> 00:19:32.171
like, you know, four
year degree required.

00:19:32.171 --> 00:19:35.307
And so what this has done
is forced a lot of people

00:19:35.307 --> 00:19:37.376
who just wanna have
a middle-class life

00:19:37.376 --> 00:19:40.312 align:start position:0%
into the university system,
they've acquired a lot of debt,

00:19:40.312 --> 00:19:41.413
they've of course acquired

00:19:41.413 --> 00:19:43.215 align:start size:88% position:13%
a lot of indoctrination
along the way,

00:19:43.215 --> 00:19:45.617
like the left is in some ways

00:19:45.617 --> 00:19:49.454 align:start position:0%
like in this evil Machiavellian
sense, sort of genius

00:19:49.454 --> 00:19:53.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they've made the most
left-leaning institutions

00:19:53.258 --> 00:19:56.195 align:start size:88% position:13%
the gatekeeper of a good
life in this society.

00:19:56.195 --> 00:19:58.864
Conservatives have got to
find an alternative to this

00:19:58.864 --> 00:20:01.066
because if we keep on
forcing all of our kids

00:20:01.066 --> 00:20:03.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
to go to four year colleges,
we shouldn't be surprised

00:20:03.602 --> 00:20:05.704 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we keep on losing
the culture war.

00:20:05.704 --> 00:20:07.039
- Yeah, I always describe it

00:20:07.039 --> 00:20:09.308
as the doctor in the
original "Alien" movie.

00:20:09.308 --> 00:20:12.444
You know, he kind of
admires the pure evil,

00:20:12.444 --> 00:20:16.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
the complete, remorseless
evil of the alien.

00:20:16.215 --> 00:20:17.883
He knows it's evil,
but he's kinda like,

00:20:17.883 --> 00:20:20.552
hey, you gotta give
the devil his due.

00:20:20.552 --> 00:20:24.389
But so all that being said,
you also have a VC firm,

00:20:24.389 --> 00:20:27.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you're actually getting
in the fight right now

00:20:27.593 --> 00:20:29.828
by getting involved with Rumble.

00:20:29.828 --> 00:20:30.896
Can you talk about
that a little bit?

00:20:30.896 --> 00:20:32.130
- Yeah, that's right.

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:34.967
Yeah, so we launched our
VC firm in Cincinnati.

00:20:34.967 --> 00:20:37.603 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I'd been in VC,
the industry for a few years,

00:20:37.603 --> 00:20:40.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
I always wanted to do
something in my hometown,

00:20:40.672 --> 00:20:44.810 align:start size:97% position:3%
so we launched it as a
Cincinnati-based firm in 2019,

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:46.044
you know, myself, a partner,

00:20:46.044 --> 00:20:48.614 align:start size:88% position:13%
and a couple of our
employees who are great,

00:20:48.614 --> 00:20:50.349
and we like on the one hand,

00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:51.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're just a classic
VC firm, right?

00:20:51.984 --> 00:20:53.986
We try to invest in
really good companies

00:20:53.986 --> 00:20:55.287
and help them grow.

00:20:55.287 --> 00:20:56.555
You know, one of the companies

00:20:56.555 --> 00:20:57.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I'm most proud
of that we invested in

00:20:57.589 --> 00:20:59.725
is a company called App Harvest,

00:20:59.725 --> 00:21:01.960 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is an agricultural
technology company

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:03.695
in Eastern Kentucky,

00:21:03.695 --> 00:21:06.198
now has a close to $2
billion market cap,

00:21:06.198 --> 00:21:10.168
when we invested it was really
just in its formative stages,

00:21:10.168 --> 00:21:11.603
so a lot of good
stuff happening,

00:21:11.603 --> 00:21:13.205 align:start size:88% position:13%
but yeah, one of the
companies I'm most proud

00:21:13.205 --> 00:21:15.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we got involved
with was Rumble.

00:21:15.374 --> 00:21:17.743
And, I know you know
a lot about Rumble,

00:21:17.743 --> 00:21:20.312
but the thinking
there was actually

00:21:20.312 --> 00:21:21.480
we were sort of looking at

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.949 align:start size:88% position:13%
a lot of the alternative
tech platforms

00:21:23.949 --> 00:21:25.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the wake of January
the sixth, right?

00:21:25.717 --> 00:21:27.185 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like, okay,
this is pretty scary,

00:21:27.185 --> 00:21:28.720
the president of
the United States

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.889
just got booted off
of social media,

00:21:30.889 --> 00:21:32.958 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
maybe these companies
are too powerful.

00:21:32.958 --> 00:21:35.127 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
You know, let's look at
Gab, let's look at Parlor,

00:21:35.127 --> 00:21:37.629 line:0%
let's look at a lot of the
companies that are out there

00:21:37.629 --> 00:21:40.465 line:0%
that are actually providing
something like free speech

00:21:40.465 --> 00:21:43.035 align:start size:88% position:13%
to people who wanna live
in the digital world,

00:21:43.035 --> 00:21:44.903
and, you know, liked
a lot of the companies

00:21:44.903 --> 00:21:46.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we looked at, couldn't
quite get comfortable

00:21:46.705 --> 00:21:48.940
that they were actually
robust enough technology

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:51.076
to withstand what eventually,

00:21:51.076 --> 00:21:52.644 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, the
de-platforming incident

00:21:52.644 --> 00:21:54.813
that came against Parlor that
effectively shut the site down

00:21:54.813 --> 00:21:57.716
for a couple of months, and
so, but just kept on looking

00:21:57.716 --> 00:22:00.986 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we finally met Chris,
who's the CEO and the founder

00:22:00.986 --> 00:22:03.088 align:start size:84% position:16%
of Rumble who I
know you know well,

00:22:03.088 --> 00:22:07.326
and started like talking
to Chris and thinking,

00:22:07.326 --> 00:22:09.561 align:start size:84% position:16%
okay, so, you
know, first of all,

00:22:09.561 --> 00:22:11.563 align:start size:88% position:13%
their user metric
numbers are incredible,

00:22:11.563 --> 00:22:13.699
they're growing at a
really remarkable clip,

00:22:13.699 --> 00:22:15.634
they've never had an
infusion of capital

00:22:15.634 --> 00:22:18.570
so they've sort of done all
of this growth organically,

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.807
but also Chris had
built the platform

00:22:21.807 --> 00:22:25.277
not to be like a conservative
website or a liberal website,

00:22:25.277 --> 00:22:27.979
but just to be a true
alternative website.

00:22:27.979 --> 00:22:31.016 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like the technology was
robust, we felt like, well,

00:22:31.016 --> 00:22:33.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
if AWS tries to
de-platform this company,

00:22:33.218 --> 00:22:35.020
they're not gonna be
able to be successful

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:36.154
because of the way that Chris

00:22:36.154 --> 00:22:37.856 align:start size:84% position:16%
had built the
underlying technology,

00:22:37.856 --> 00:22:39.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so just eventually
got really excited

00:22:39.891 --> 00:22:43.128
about what they were doing,
made a big investment there,

00:22:43.128 --> 00:22:44.830
brought Peter Thiel
along into the deal,

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:47.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
he also made a big
investment into the company,

00:22:47.132 --> 00:22:50.602
and now it's growing at
a really amazing clip

00:22:50.602 --> 00:22:52.637
and we think that this could be

00:22:52.637 --> 00:22:57.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
a true independent, apolitical
alternative to big tech.

00:22:59.077 --> 00:23:00.579
- Right, so I don't wanna
say too much at the moment,

00:23:00.579 --> 00:23:03.315
but I am having dinner in
Miami with Chris on Friday,

00:23:03.315 --> 00:23:04.850
so I'll leave that part there.

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.385
But in essence,

00:23:06.385 --> 00:23:09.721
is the goal that you guys
sort of wanna replace AWS,

00:23:09.721 --> 00:23:11.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you guys would
basically say,

00:23:11.656 --> 00:23:13.725
hey, we've got all of
the infrastructure.

00:23:13.725 --> 00:23:14.926
Chris is an engineer,

00:23:14.926 --> 00:23:16.561 align:start size:88% position:13%
he's been building this
thing for 10 years,

00:23:16.561 --> 00:23:17.896 align:start size:81% position:19%
like we've got
the infrastructure

00:23:17.896 --> 00:23:20.899
so that you won't just
be blown up like Parlor.

00:23:20.899 --> 00:23:22.868
Because you're right,
that was the moment

00:23:22.868 --> 00:23:25.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
where it was like everyone
realized this thing,

00:23:25.036 --> 00:23:28.039
just the severity
of the situation

00:23:28.039 --> 00:23:29.708
just went through the roof.

00:23:29.708 --> 00:23:30.976
- That's right, yeah.

00:23:30.976 --> 00:23:32.244
More even than Trump
being de-platformed,

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:34.246
Parlor being effectively
kicked off the internet

00:23:34.246 --> 00:23:36.281
was really radicalizing to me

00:23:36.281 --> 00:23:38.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to a lot of
other people as well.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:40.118
You know, certainly
one of the options,

00:23:40.118 --> 00:23:41.620
I'll put it this way.

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:43.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
One of the options I think
for Rumble is to become

00:23:43.555 --> 00:23:47.926 align:start size:97% position:3%
a long-term, apolitical,
true free speech, AWS, right?

00:23:47.926 --> 00:23:49.895
The sort of online web platform

00:23:49.895 --> 00:23:51.363
that's not gonna de-platform you

00:23:51.363 --> 00:23:54.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you say things
that the mob doesn't like.

00:23:54.433 --> 00:23:55.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
But one of the
things I've learned

00:23:55.667 --> 00:23:57.068
working in the technology sector

00:23:57.068 --> 00:23:58.503
is you always try
to have a good plan

00:23:58.503 --> 00:23:59.971
and you execute against it,

00:23:59.971 --> 00:24:01.807
but there are a lot
of forks in the road,

00:24:01.807 --> 00:24:06.244 align:start position:0%
and I think Rumble has 10
different pathways to the moon.

00:24:06.244 --> 00:24:07.546
Like this is not a company

00:24:07.546 --> 00:24:10.415 align:start size:84% position:16%
that just has one real
option to go after,

00:24:10.415 --> 00:24:11.917
and what I think actually,

00:24:11.917 --> 00:24:14.119
you know, there's always
challenges with companies

00:24:14.119 --> 00:24:16.755 align:start size:97% position:3%
and one of the challenges
with Rumble that I think about

00:24:16.755 --> 00:24:19.925 align:start size:88% position:13%
is they have such a
big market opportunity,

00:24:19.925 --> 00:24:22.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
they have so much they could
do, that I think focusing

00:24:22.894 --> 00:24:25.397 align:start size:84% position:16%
and picking the right
strategic pathway

00:24:25.397 --> 00:24:27.098
is almost gonna be
the biggest challenge

00:24:27.098 --> 00:24:30.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the company, again,
they're just doing really well

00:24:30.669 --> 00:24:32.838
and they've got a
lot of real upside.

00:24:32.838 --> 00:24:35.273
- Right, and not to do too
much of a commercial here,

00:24:35.273 --> 00:24:38.477
but you guys had the Trump
rally a couple of days ago

00:24:38.477 --> 00:24:41.713
livestreamed on Rumble,
over 500,000 people.

00:24:41.713 --> 00:24:44.449 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think Biden's speech
yesterday or the day before,

00:24:44.449 --> 00:24:46.151
18,000 people on YouTube.

00:24:46.151 --> 00:24:48.520
So, something good's
going on over there.

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:50.589
- Yeah, that's exactly
right, 500,000 people.

00:24:50.589 --> 00:24:53.058
And being able to handle
that amount of traffic

00:24:53.058 --> 00:24:55.827
as a new company is not easy

00:24:55.827 --> 00:24:57.896
so we were really
pleased with it.

00:24:57.896 --> 00:25:02.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so as this ecosystem
kinda comes together,

00:25:02.033 --> 00:25:03.468
are there other pieces

00:25:03.468 --> 00:25:05.604
that you think we need
to be thinking about

00:25:05.604 --> 00:25:07.405
that maybe we're
not thinking about?

00:25:08.907 --> 00:25:12.244
- Well, the thing that
we're not thinking about,

00:25:12.244 --> 00:25:13.678
I think is just how--

00:25:13.678 --> 00:25:15.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You know, payment
processors, et cetera.

00:25:15.647 --> 00:25:19.184
- You know, exactly,
is how truly unstable

00:25:19.184 --> 00:25:21.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
the entire online
ecosystem is, right?

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:24.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
You mentioned
payment processors.

00:25:24.356 --> 00:25:28.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's sort of the Domain
Registration Services,

00:25:28.326 --> 00:25:29.828
that's a big question.

00:25:30.996 --> 00:25:33.331 align:start size:84% position:16%
There are sort of, you
know, CDN services.

00:25:33.331 --> 00:25:35.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's a big question
mark that I have.

00:25:35.534 --> 00:25:38.370 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, if you get
really far down the line

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.172
and the tech companies feel
extraordinarily powerful,

00:25:41.172 --> 00:25:43.975
do you get to a point where
if you type in a website

00:25:43.975 --> 00:25:47.646 align:start position:0%
on a mobile browser, the
browser won't actually resolve?

00:25:47.646 --> 00:25:50.615 align:start position:0%
Like that's sort of the hallmark
of a free and open internet

00:25:50.615 --> 00:25:54.352 align:start position:0%
is that if you type in a
website, you go to that website,

00:25:54.352 --> 00:25:56.121
do the browsers themselves

00:25:56.121 --> 00:25:58.223 align:start size:84% position:16%
start becoming
agents of censorship?

00:25:58.223 --> 00:26:00.025
So what's crazy about
the internet I mean,

00:26:00.025 --> 00:26:03.428
is that there are probably 40
or 50 really critical layers

00:26:03.428 --> 00:26:06.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
that make it work from sort
of payments processing,

00:26:06.164 --> 00:26:09.467
front end services, all the
way down to the backend stuff

00:26:09.467 --> 00:26:12.203 align:start size:88% position:13%
that 99% of people
don't see or care about,

00:26:12.203 --> 00:26:15.473
but at each level there
are only a few companies

00:26:15.473 --> 00:26:17.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
that actually control
things, right?

00:26:17.442 --> 00:26:21.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so it's not inconceivable
that you have an internet

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.381
where people can't
have access to it

00:26:23.381 --> 00:26:25.417 align:start size:88% position:13%
if they're not
sufficiently left wing.

00:26:25.417 --> 00:26:28.186
That's sort of a big
concern that I have

00:26:28.186 --> 00:26:30.822
and I think that there
are a lot of good folks,

00:26:30.822 --> 00:26:34.559
you know, a lot of good firms
that are working on ensuring

00:26:34.559 --> 00:26:36.895
there are alternatives
that are truly committed

00:26:36.895 --> 00:26:40.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
to free speech, so I'm
somewhat optimistic

00:26:40.899 --> 00:26:42.601 align:start position:0%
that some of these
alternatives are coming online,

00:26:42.601 --> 00:26:44.869
but I do in some way
see this as like a race

00:26:44.869 --> 00:26:47.439
against the future,
because the left is,

00:26:47.439 --> 00:26:49.107
as you know, is not
becoming less crazy,

00:26:49.107 --> 00:26:51.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's actually
becoming more crazy.

00:26:51.042 --> 00:26:53.878
And, you know, once they
control the information,

00:26:53.878 --> 00:26:55.513 align:start size:88% position:13%
they really control
the way all of us think

00:26:55.513 --> 00:26:56.982
and that's pretty much it.

00:26:56.982 --> 00:26:58.416
- Yeah, is that part of
the problem right there,

00:26:58.416 --> 00:27:00.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
that there's just
sort of an asymmetry

00:27:00.518 --> 00:27:03.154
of who likes to use power?

00:27:03.154 --> 00:27:04.889
I've been talking about
that a lot on the show,

00:27:04.889 --> 00:27:08.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
just that we don't
really wanna use power,

00:27:08.059 --> 00:27:09.327
even in this conversation.

00:27:09.327 --> 00:27:11.997
It's like, ah, you know,
competition, government,

00:27:11.997 --> 00:27:13.732
it's like we're sort of okay

00:27:13.732 --> 00:27:15.767 align:start size:84% position:16%
with competing ideas
being out there,

00:27:15.767 --> 00:27:19.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
where when they have the
power, we know what they do.

00:27:19.537 --> 00:27:20.872
- Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:27:20.872 --> 00:27:22.273
You know, people always
ask me the difference

00:27:22.273 --> 00:27:23.408
between left and right.

00:27:23.408 --> 00:27:24.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
Is it small government,
big government,

00:27:24.876 --> 00:27:27.412 align:start size:97% position:3%
social conservative,
progressive, what have you,

00:27:27.412 --> 00:27:29.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I always say, look,
the big difference

00:27:29.047 --> 00:27:32.250
between the left and right is
the left loves to use power

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:34.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the right is
terrified of using power.

00:27:34.486 --> 00:27:37.355 align:start size:88% position:13%
And you know, my friend
Richard Hananiah,

00:27:37.355 --> 00:27:39.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
who's a really interesting
thinker on this,

00:27:39.391 --> 00:27:41.192
he says the left
loves to use power

00:27:41.192 --> 00:27:43.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they actually
care about politics more.

00:27:43.361 --> 00:27:44.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, you know,
you look at polling

00:27:44.863 --> 00:27:46.798
and the right just doesn't
care as much about politics

00:27:46.798 --> 00:27:48.033
as the left does.

00:27:48.033 --> 00:27:49.401
I think, unfortunately
we're in an environment

00:27:49.401 --> 00:27:50.669
where we have to get over it

00:27:50.669 --> 00:27:53.271
because if we're not
willing to use power

00:27:53.271 --> 00:27:54.606
to push back against the left,

00:27:54.606 --> 00:27:56.508
I think that power
is gonna come for us,

00:27:56.508 --> 00:27:58.076
it's gonna come for
our livelihoods,

00:27:58.076 --> 00:27:59.477
it's gonna come
for our families,

00:27:59.477 --> 00:28:01.413 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's gonna come eventually,
it already started,

00:28:01.413 --> 00:28:04.215 align:start size:84% position:16%
coming for our
variability to speak

00:28:04.215 --> 00:28:05.984
and to think for ourselves.

00:28:05.984 --> 00:28:09.954
And so I agree, the left has
a basic instinctive advantage

00:28:09.954 --> 00:28:11.790
because they love to use power,

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:13.158
but we're just gonna
have to get over it

00:28:13.158 --> 00:28:14.359
or we're gonna find ourselves

00:28:14.359 --> 00:28:17.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
without really a real
Republic anymore.

00:28:17.662 --> 00:28:20.365
- Yeah, do you see
any sort of version

00:28:20.365 --> 00:28:22.167
of any of this ecosystem,

00:28:22.167 --> 00:28:25.103
where there has to be some
regulations around speech?

00:28:25.103 --> 00:28:27.272
This is where the gatekeeping
conversation always comes in.

00:28:27.272 --> 00:28:29.974
You know, are you gonna let
Antifa coordinate on there

00:28:29.974 --> 00:28:33.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
on one side versus
neo-Nazis on another side

00:28:33.244 --> 00:28:34.846
or something like that?

00:28:34.846 --> 00:28:37.682
- You know, obviously
there's some regulation

00:28:37.682 --> 00:28:40.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
in every sphere, and I tend
to think that, you know, look,

00:28:40.418 --> 00:28:43.588
we've had a First Amendment
in our country for 245 years

00:28:43.588 --> 00:28:45.724
and it's worked pretty well,

00:28:45.724 --> 00:28:48.226
and the basic outline
of the First Amendment

00:28:48.226 --> 00:28:49.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that you're allowed
to speak your mind,

00:28:49.961 --> 00:28:52.430
you're allowed to say the
things that you wanna say,

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:54.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
so long as you effectively
don't incite violence

00:28:54.766 --> 00:28:56.267
against other people, right?

00:28:56.267 --> 00:28:58.636
There are very narrow limits
on what you're allowed to say,

00:28:58.636 --> 00:29:01.673
but there are some limits
and I think that's fine.

00:29:01.673 --> 00:29:03.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think that
should be the hallmark

00:29:03.675 --> 00:29:07.078 align:start size:88% position:13%
to how we think about
the regulation of speech

00:29:07.078 --> 00:29:11.649
is we should be open
to hearing a debate

00:29:11.649 --> 00:29:13.084
so long as it doesn't run a foul

00:29:13.084 --> 00:29:15.053
of the basic principles
of the First Amendment.

00:29:15.053 --> 00:29:17.222
Now importantly,
like this doesn't,

00:29:17.222 --> 00:29:19.057
you know, I think a lot
of folks on the left

00:29:19.057 --> 00:29:21.359
like to retreat and
say, well, you know,

00:29:21.359 --> 00:29:23.728
are you not allowed to sort of
regulate a school curriculum?

00:29:23.728 --> 00:29:26.397 align:start size:97% position:3%
For example, if you don't
want to teach four-year-olds

00:29:26.397 --> 00:29:29.167
or eight-year-olds that
they should hate America

00:29:29.167 --> 00:29:30.301
and that America
is a fundamentally

00:29:30.301 --> 00:29:32.270
white supremacist country,

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:34.072
is that like a violation
of the First Amendment?

00:29:34.072 --> 00:29:35.607 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think, no, of
course, it isn't, right?

00:29:35.607 --> 00:29:40.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've long understood
that the way we treat kids

00:29:40.178 --> 00:29:41.780
is different from the
way we treat adults

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:43.114
and the purpose of our schools

00:29:43.114 --> 00:29:45.750
is to form people,
form young minds

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:49.154
for future leadership
and future citizenship,

00:29:49.154 --> 00:29:50.255
so I think the First Amendment

00:29:50.255 --> 00:29:51.589
doesn't apply everywhere, right?

00:29:51.589 --> 00:29:53.491 align:start size:97% position:3%
It applies to a 19-year-old
much differently

00:29:53.491 --> 00:29:55.360
than it applies to
a three-year-old,

00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:57.929
but I think that should
be our guiding principle.

00:29:57.929 --> 00:30:00.999 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, that's actually exactly
where I want it to go next.

00:30:00.999 --> 00:30:02.433
All this critical
race theory stuff,

00:30:02.433 --> 00:30:05.103 align:start position:0%
and that it's being pushed
back against in certain states,

00:30:05.103 --> 00:30:06.805 align:start size:81% position:19%
what's going on
in Ohio with this?

00:30:06.805 --> 00:30:08.773 align:start size:97% position:3%
Is that one of the states that
they're making any headway

00:30:08.773 --> 00:30:10.608
with getting some of this
stuff out of the schools?

00:30:10.608 --> 00:30:11.976
- Yeah, it really does
feel like we're making

00:30:11.976 --> 00:30:13.678 align:start size:88% position:13%
a lot of headway in Ohio
as a matter of fact.

00:30:13.678 --> 00:30:16.080
I mean, we're starting to see

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:17.982
even, you know, I live
in southwestern Ohio,

00:30:17.982 --> 00:30:21.352 align:start position:0%
biggest city here is Cincinnati,
and I'm starting to see

00:30:21.352 --> 00:30:23.922
stuff in some of the suburban
Cincinnati school districts

00:30:23.922 --> 00:30:26.925
where parents are starting to
push back against this stuff.

00:30:26.925 --> 00:30:28.960
You know, you see them
starting to mobilize

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
to take over some
local school boards,

00:30:30.728 --> 00:30:32.530
you have stay-at-home moms

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:34.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
who never really
cared about politics,

00:30:34.098 --> 00:30:36.734 align:start size:97% position:3%
who are thinking about running
for a local school board,

00:30:36.734 --> 00:30:39.170 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you also have some
curriculum stuff happening,

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:41.840 align:start position:0%
some of the state organizations
are really pushing back

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:44.175 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
against some of the
curriculum that's coming

00:30:44.175 --> 00:30:45.777 line:0%
unfortunately, from
our universities

00:30:45.777 --> 00:30:49.080 line:0%
and some of our state
leaders saying, look,

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:50.882
you shouldn't be teaching
kids to hate themselves

00:30:50.882 --> 00:30:52.350
or hate their own country.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:54.485 align:start position:0%
You know, one of the most
heartbreaking stories I've heard

00:30:54.485 --> 00:30:56.921 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the last few
weeks was from a dad

00:30:56.921 --> 00:30:59.791
who pulled his fifth
grader out of school

00:30:59.791 --> 00:31:02.060 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a suburban Cincinnati
school district,

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:04.729
and, you know, we often
talk about this stuff

00:31:04.729 --> 00:31:06.397
in two abstract terms, right?

00:31:06.397 --> 00:31:09.367
Like critical race theory
is itself an abstract term,

00:31:09.367 --> 00:31:11.603
but this guy pulled his
daughter out of school

00:31:11.603 --> 00:31:14.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
because she was coming
home really sad every day,

00:31:14.672 --> 00:31:17.008
being taught that she was
effectively a bad person

00:31:17.008 --> 00:31:18.743
for having white skin,

00:31:18.743 --> 00:31:20.812
and you think about
the struggles we've
had in this country

00:31:20.812 --> 00:31:22.413
and what we've been fighting for

00:31:22.413 --> 00:31:24.616
and I think progressing towards,

00:31:24.616 --> 00:31:27.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's like aren't we supposed
to think about people

00:31:27.552 --> 00:31:29.053
based not on their skin color?

00:31:29.053 --> 00:31:31.656 align:start position:0%
Aren't we supposed to be
teaching our kids to look deeper

00:31:31.656 --> 00:31:34.826
than skin color in how they
judge and evaluate people?

00:31:34.826 --> 00:31:37.629
And this just feels like a
really radical step backwards

00:31:37.629 --> 00:31:38.830
in the wrong direction

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:41.032 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's actually
making our kids unhappy.

00:31:41.032 --> 00:31:44.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's what really
bothers me about this.

00:31:44.102 --> 00:31:45.603
- Yeah, so you're old school,

00:31:45.603 --> 00:31:48.106 align:start size:97% position:3%
you think that that MLK
guy was pretty decent, huh?

00:31:48.106 --> 00:31:49.641
(J.D. laughs)

00:31:49.641 --> 00:31:50.909
- You know, I think the
idea that we judge people

00:31:50.909 --> 00:31:52.443
by the content of
their character

00:31:52.443 --> 00:31:55.113
is a pretty good principle to
live by and it's unfortunate

00:31:55.113 --> 00:31:58.683
that the left seems unwilling
to even follow it these days.

00:31:58.683 --> 00:32:03.588
- Yeah, so what other
policies or platforms

00:32:05.023 --> 00:32:07.425
that you're running on that
are unique to Ohio right now?

00:32:08.593 --> 00:32:10.528 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You know, the big
thing we're focused on

00:32:10.528 --> 00:32:13.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
is the tech issue,
the immigration issue.

00:32:13.998 --> 00:32:17.769
You know, people think,
I think people understand

00:32:17.769 --> 00:32:19.304
the immigration question

00:32:19.304 --> 00:32:22.006
as fundamentally about law and
order, and it is that, right?

00:32:22.006 --> 00:32:23.508
You don't have a real country

00:32:23.508 --> 00:32:25.743
unless you have a southern
border with enforced limits,

00:32:27.011 --> 00:32:28.980
but in Ohio, one of the things

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:32.750
the southern border means to
us is the addiction epidemic

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:34.619
gets much, much worse
when you don't control

00:32:34.619 --> 00:32:35.954
what's going on at
the southern border.

00:32:35.954 --> 00:32:39.557
So for example, a few weeks ago,

00:32:39.557 --> 00:32:41.092
Biden administration reported

00:32:41.092 --> 00:32:43.728
that you've seen fentanyl
seizures go up four times

00:32:43.728 --> 00:32:45.196
over the same period last year,

00:32:45.196 --> 00:32:46.631 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I think Trump,
to his credit,

00:32:46.631 --> 00:32:48.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
had a lot of control
over the southern border.

00:32:48.433 --> 00:32:50.969
That's what kills people
in our communities, right?

00:32:50.969 --> 00:32:52.704
So eventually that fentanyl

00:32:52.704 --> 00:32:55.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
is gonna start making it to
southwestern Ohio communities,

00:32:55.139 --> 00:32:56.975
it's gonna mean more dead bodies

00:32:56.975 --> 00:32:58.476
from the addiction epidemic,

00:32:58.476 --> 00:33:01.479
and people just, you
know, I always hate

00:33:01.479 --> 00:33:03.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
when the immigration
issue is framed

00:33:03.281 --> 00:33:05.717
as about xenophobia or racism.

00:33:05.717 --> 00:33:09.320 align:start position:0%
Like you've got a grandma who's
taking care of a grandbaby

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:11.689
because her own daughter
died of a heroin overdose,

00:33:11.689 --> 00:33:13.157 align:start size:84% position:16%
she doesn't care about
the southern border

00:33:13.157 --> 00:33:14.392
'cause she doesn't
like brown people,

00:33:14.392 --> 00:33:16.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
she cares about
the southern border

00:33:16.094 --> 00:33:17.895
because she doesn't
want her grandson

00:33:17.895 --> 00:33:20.765
to die of the same poison
that killed her daughter.

00:33:20.765 --> 00:33:22.233
Like this is just common sense.

00:33:22.233 --> 00:33:23.801
So the border's a
big thing for us,

00:33:23.801 --> 00:33:26.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
obviously the big tech
issue is a big thing for us,

00:33:26.537 --> 00:33:29.607 align:start position:0%
and the thing we haven't talked
about is I'm a big believer

00:33:29.607 --> 00:33:31.442
that unless you have
an industrial base,

00:33:31.442 --> 00:33:33.978 align:start position:0%
a manufacturing base, you don't
have a real country, right?

00:33:33.978 --> 00:33:35.913
If you can't make
pharmaceuticals,

00:33:35.913 --> 00:33:38.616 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you can't make surgical
masks for your doctors,

00:33:38.616 --> 00:33:41.452
if you can't make like
basic computer hardware

00:33:41.452 --> 00:33:44.122 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's necessary to operate
your defense industry,

00:33:44.122 --> 00:33:46.557
then you're fundamentally at
the mercy of another country.

00:33:46.557 --> 00:33:49.927 align:start position:0%
Like we're getting to the
point where six years from now,

00:33:49.927 --> 00:33:51.629 align:start size:88% position:13%
we could find ourselves
in a situation

00:33:51.629 --> 00:33:54.966
where we have the same
missiles as China does,

00:33:54.966 --> 00:33:56.567
but China's missiles work

00:33:56.567 --> 00:33:58.269 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they have
computer chips in them

00:33:58.269 --> 00:34:00.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
and our missiles don't
because the Chinese

00:34:00.271 --> 00:34:03.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
control the computer chip
industry in the entire world.

00:34:03.474 --> 00:34:06.077
Like this is really crazy
and this is unfortunately

00:34:06.077 --> 00:34:08.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
something that leadership
in both parties

00:34:08.679 --> 00:34:09.914
has been complicit in,

00:34:09.914 --> 00:34:11.849
so we're gonna talk a
lot about manufacturing

00:34:11.849 --> 00:34:14.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the manufacturing
economy in our campaign too.

00:34:14.419 --> 00:34:16.587
- Where do you think that
stuff really comes from?

00:34:16.587 --> 00:34:19.223
Like is it just that sort
of because of the success

00:34:19.223 --> 00:34:21.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
of America for
200-almost-50 years,

00:34:21.592 --> 00:34:24.262
and capitalism and the
extraordinary wealth

00:34:24.262 --> 00:34:26.697
and everything else that
we just sorta got sold off

00:34:26.697 --> 00:34:29.967
piece by piece and it's like,
it was sort of nobody's fault

00:34:29.967 --> 00:34:31.436
and everybody's fault
at the exact same time?

00:34:31.436 --> 00:34:35.606 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like there's just sort of
nobody left to fix everything.

00:34:35.606 --> 00:34:36.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I think
that's exactly right.

00:34:36.841 --> 00:34:38.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think two big
things happen is one,

00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:43.047
we felt that we could trade
our manufacturing economy

00:34:43.047 --> 00:34:44.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
for a lot of cheap
consumer goods, right?

00:34:44.982 --> 00:34:47.819
To put it as dismissively as
possible, we thought, well,

00:34:47.819 --> 00:34:50.354 align:start position:0%
you know, maybe we'll lose
millions of manufacturing jobs,

00:34:50.354 --> 00:34:52.757
but we'll gain a lot of cheap
plastic garbage from China.

00:34:52.757 --> 00:34:54.058
I think that was a trade

00:34:54.058 --> 00:34:56.027 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we should not
have made in hindsight,

00:34:56.027 --> 00:34:57.495 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I think to your
point, more importantly,

00:34:57.495 --> 00:35:00.498
we forgot I think one of
the core historical lessons

00:35:00.498 --> 00:35:03.835
of the American nation state,
which is that it was built

00:35:03.835 --> 00:35:06.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
by an American
capitalist system, right?

00:35:06.471 --> 00:35:11.342 align:start size:97% position:3%
Our government was not
overbearing, but it made sure

00:35:11.342 --> 00:35:14.545
that core American industries
could prosper and flourish

00:35:14.545 --> 00:35:16.214
in this country,
and importantly,

00:35:16.214 --> 00:35:20.251
the Japanese and the Chinese
learned from the American model

00:35:20.251 --> 00:35:22.086
in building their own
industrial economy.

00:35:22.086 --> 00:35:24.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's really a model that's
worked the world over

00:35:24.889 --> 00:35:27.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we sort of just
forgot and ignored it.

00:35:27.125 --> 00:35:30.061
We sort of decided that we
didn't really need to have

00:35:30.061 --> 00:35:31.496
certain industries,

00:35:31.496 --> 00:35:33.264
you know, the law of
competitive advantage.

00:35:33.264 --> 00:35:35.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was fine if Chinese
made this thing

00:35:35.266 --> 00:35:36.634
and the Americans
had made this thing

00:35:36.634 --> 00:35:39.904
and the Canadians
made that thing,

00:35:39.904 --> 00:35:42.507 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it turns out that
like maybe the Canadians

00:35:42.507 --> 00:35:44.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
are a little bit more
reliable than the Chinese

00:35:44.509 --> 00:35:47.445
for like complex cultural and
national security reasons,

00:35:47.445 --> 00:35:50.248 align:start size:97% position:3%
and certainly maybe Minnesota
is much more reliable

00:35:50.248 --> 00:35:53.618
than Canada or the Chinese
for some of these core needs,

00:35:53.618 --> 00:35:56.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so I think we just
stopped seeing the economy

00:35:56.888 --> 00:36:01.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
as a thing that was
sensitive to human control,

00:36:02.994 --> 00:36:05.763 align:start size:88% position:13%
we thought that it would
just work by itself,

00:36:05.763 --> 00:36:07.532
but of course, all
of our regulations,

00:36:07.532 --> 00:36:10.668
all of our tax policy, all
of our laws were affecting

00:36:10.668 --> 00:36:13.571 align:start size:97% position:3%
that market in ways we
were just kind of ignoring.

00:36:13.571 --> 00:36:15.573
- Do you think it's also
that we kinda took our eye

00:36:15.573 --> 00:36:17.408 align:start size:84% position:16%
off the ball with four
years of screaming

00:36:17.408 --> 00:36:19.477
that Trump was a Russian plant,

00:36:19.477 --> 00:36:22.213
while China was actually
doing a lot of the stuff

00:36:22.213 --> 00:36:23.514
that was gonna get us here?

00:36:23.514 --> 00:36:24.949
- Yeah, that's definitely right.

00:36:24.949 --> 00:36:28.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, we definitely learned
over the last four years

00:36:28.452 --> 00:36:32.757
that the media especially
can be easily distracted.

00:36:32.757 --> 00:36:34.425
It's like, you know,
look a squirrel, right?

00:36:34.425 --> 00:36:36.127 align:start size:84% position:16%
Look, Russia, look,
Vladimir Putin,

00:36:36.127 --> 00:36:38.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we all have these
really serious problems.

00:36:38.029 --> 00:36:39.497
Look, racism, right?

00:36:39.497 --> 00:36:40.965
I mean, you couldn't even
talk about the origins

00:36:40.965 --> 00:36:45.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the Chinese coronavirus
for a long time

00:36:45.102 --> 00:36:48.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
until it became
almost idiotic not to,

00:36:48.406 --> 00:36:51.209
because that was considered
racist to ask the origin.

00:36:51.209 --> 00:36:52.710
So I think, unfortunately,

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:54.712
we have a leadership
class in this country.

00:36:54.712 --> 00:36:56.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
You see it in the businesses,
you see it in the media,

00:36:56.814 --> 00:36:58.583
you certainly see it
in the government,

00:36:58.583 --> 00:37:00.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is just
completely unwilling

00:37:00.117 --> 00:37:03.454
to talk about real problems
and uses these distractions

00:37:03.454 --> 00:37:05.590
so that we basically
don't notice the fact

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:07.391
that they're all failing us.

00:37:07.391 --> 00:37:09.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So what do we do about
the mainstream media?

00:37:09.594 --> 00:37:11.796
Do we just let it, do we
let it die at this point?

00:37:11.796 --> 00:37:13.331
Almost every day on the show

00:37:13.331 --> 00:37:16.467
I have to do some nonsensical
story about what CNN butchered

00:37:16.467 --> 00:37:18.703
or what "Washington
Post" lied about,

00:37:18.703 --> 00:37:20.938
and then I always pose the
question to my audience,

00:37:20.938 --> 00:37:23.307
I'm like, should I just
start ignoring this stuff

00:37:23.307 --> 00:37:27.478 align:start size:94% position:6%
because we know their
numbers are bottoming out

00:37:27.478 --> 00:37:29.680
or do we have to keep
calling attention to it

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:32.683 align:start position:0%
because they still are
brainwashing X amount of people?

00:37:32.683 --> 00:37:33.951
- I think it's a
little bit of both.

00:37:33.951 --> 00:37:36.220
I think that we have to
try to extract ourselves

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:38.990 align:start position:0%
from reliance on the mainstream
media as much as possible,

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:41.559
but I also think it is
important to recognize,

00:37:41.559 --> 00:37:44.195
like your show probably
has way more viewers

00:37:44.195 --> 00:37:47.064
than any CNN show on
any given day, right?

00:37:47.064 --> 00:37:49.900
- I've got one guy in this
room with me, that's it.

00:37:49.900 --> 00:37:51.168
(J.D. laughs)

00:37:51.168 --> 00:37:53.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's it and my
dog's under the table.

00:37:53.404 --> 00:37:54.238
- That's right.

00:37:54.238 --> 00:37:55.406
(J.D. laughs)

00:37:55.406 --> 00:37:57.642 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we are actually
benefiting in some ways

00:37:57.642 --> 00:38:00.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
from the rise of
alternative media sources.

00:38:00.044 --> 00:38:04.148
I mean, I think actually
where I worry about this

00:38:04.148 --> 00:38:05.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
as sort of the
fusion of government

00:38:05.916 --> 00:38:07.585
and media and big
business, right?

00:38:07.585 --> 00:38:09.820 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like the "Washington Post,"
you should understand

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:12.256 align:start size:94% position:6%
is effectively the
mouthpiece of Jeff Bezos.

00:38:12.256 --> 00:38:14.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, a lot of our biggest
newspapers and magazines

00:38:14.959 --> 00:38:18.062 align:start size:88% position:13%
are effectively
controlled by business,

00:38:18.062 --> 00:38:20.765
and in some ways influenced
by governmental interests.

00:38:20.765 --> 00:38:23.868
That sort of unholy alliance
is what I really worry about,

00:38:23.868 --> 00:38:27.972
is like, okay, so Jeff Bezos
owns the "Washington Post,"

00:38:27.972 --> 00:38:31.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
he just effectively
lobbied the U.S. Congress

00:38:31.309 --> 00:38:34.312
to cancel a grant given to one
of his business competitors.

00:38:34.312 --> 00:38:36.213
This just happened in
the last couple of days

00:38:36.213 --> 00:38:38.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
for a space company,
Blue Origin,

00:38:38.282 --> 00:38:39.884 align:start size:84% position:16%
where now they've
canceled the contract,

00:38:39.884 --> 00:38:42.119 align:start size:84% position:16%
they may re-award
the contract to him,

00:38:42.119 --> 00:38:43.387
and you sort of start to wonder,

00:38:43.387 --> 00:38:45.456
like isn't this a
really icky system

00:38:45.456 --> 00:38:47.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
where you have media
cooperating with government,

00:38:47.558 --> 00:38:50.461
cooperating with business to
control the flow of information

00:38:50.461 --> 00:38:52.296
and control which
decisions get made?

00:38:52.296 --> 00:38:55.166
I actually think you can
probably break the circuit

00:38:55.166 --> 00:38:57.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
and in interesting ways,
and we should try to do that

00:38:57.835 --> 00:38:59.637
because yes, the
media is weaker,

00:38:59.637 --> 00:39:01.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's still
incredibly powerful.

00:39:01.872 --> 00:39:04.241
- Yeah, so is that just
through competition then?

00:39:04.241 --> 00:39:05.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, this is the
breaking of the circuit

00:39:05.876 --> 00:39:07.211 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we're gonna
build all this stuff

00:39:07.211 --> 00:39:09.847 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then people, they'll
have an off ramp?

00:39:09.847 --> 00:39:11.649 align:start position:0%
- You know, I think it's a
little bit through competition.

00:39:11.649 --> 00:39:14.952 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I've heard
proposals that suggest

00:39:14.952 --> 00:39:16.921
that like part of the reason

00:39:16.921 --> 00:39:20.591
the media is a little bit less
crazy in Europe for example,

00:39:20.591 --> 00:39:23.027
is that they have more of a
historical First Amendment,

00:39:23.027 --> 00:39:24.462
whereas in America

00:39:24.462 --> 00:39:26.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have this weird,
trumped up First Amendment

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:28.833 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the media can
effectively lie about people

00:39:28.833 --> 00:39:30.434
without any real consequences,

00:39:30.434 --> 00:39:32.970
and so maybe we need to
sort of go back to an era

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:35.573
where there's a little
bit more consequence

00:39:35.573 --> 00:39:37.842
if the media just tells an
outright lie about somebody.

00:39:37.842 --> 00:39:39.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, you know, if
I'm a private citizen,

00:39:39.677 --> 00:39:41.379
I tell a lie about you

00:39:41.379 --> 00:39:42.813
that causes you to
lose your business,

00:39:42.813 --> 00:39:44.181
you can sue me for defamation.

00:39:44.181 --> 00:39:45.783 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's basically impossible
to sue the media

00:39:45.783 --> 00:39:47.351
for defamation these days,

00:39:47.351 --> 00:39:51.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
so there maybe are policy
changes to be made there too.

00:39:51.021 --> 00:39:56.026
You know, I wouldn't sort
of fully go down that road

00:39:57.395 --> 00:39:59.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
just yet because I do
think the alternative media

00:39:59.029 --> 00:40:01.966
is actually rising in a way
that gives me some optimism.

00:40:01.966 --> 00:40:03.868 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, you know, to your
point a couple of weeks ago,

00:40:03.868 --> 00:40:05.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
someone in the "New
York Times," once again,

00:40:05.636 --> 00:40:08.205
basically called me
a white supremacist

00:40:08.205 --> 00:40:10.141 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I spoke to a lawyer
just for a second,

00:40:10.141 --> 00:40:11.909
and I've been through
this, just like you,

00:40:11.909 --> 00:40:13.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've been through
this a million times,

00:40:13.177 --> 00:40:14.945 align:start size:88% position:13%
and she's a great
First Amendment lawyer,

00:40:14.945 --> 00:40:16.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
and she was
basically like, look,

00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:18.416
you could dump a ton
of money into this,

00:40:18.416 --> 00:40:20.618
but these laws are so tight

00:40:20.618 --> 00:40:23.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
that most likely they
have more money than you

00:40:23.220 --> 00:40:24.455
and this isn't gonna go anywhere

00:40:24.455 --> 00:40:25.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's just gonna
be a headache.

00:40:25.656 --> 00:40:26.590
And you know what?

00:40:26.590 --> 00:40:28.359
Somehow we just, we all survived

00:40:28.359 --> 00:40:30.928
being called white
supremacists somehow.

00:40:30.928 --> 00:40:32.163
- Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:40:32.163 --> 00:40:33.998
I've had similar conversations.

00:40:33.998 --> 00:40:35.433
I've been called a
white supremacist

00:40:35.433 --> 00:40:36.700
by the "Washington Post"

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:40.504 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it is really crazy
how it's impossible

00:40:40.504 --> 00:40:45.476
for people to face
any consequences for
this stuff, right?

00:40:46.644 --> 00:40:48.846 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's kind of this
crazy wild, wild west

00:40:48.846 --> 00:40:50.514
where there's just no real law.

00:40:50.514 --> 00:40:52.683
I mean, you remember the
Covington Catholic kid

00:40:52.683 --> 00:40:54.084
who was effectively slandered

00:40:54.084 --> 00:40:57.254 align:start size:97% position:3%
by every mainstream
journalist all over the world?

00:40:57.254 --> 00:40:59.156 align:start size:84% position:16%
That kid, fortunately,
as I understand,

00:40:59.156 --> 00:41:00.858
has gotten some settlement,

00:41:00.858 --> 00:41:02.726
but even that kid,
my understanding

00:41:02.726 --> 00:41:04.795
is like it was not totally clear

00:41:04.795 --> 00:41:07.631 line:0%
that this 17-year-old boy
would have any recourse,

00:41:07.631 --> 00:41:10.568 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
even though the entire
mainstream press
came against him.

00:41:10.568 --> 00:41:12.102 line:0%
That's pretty depressing stuff.

00:41:12.102 --> 00:41:13.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, not only the
mainstream press,

00:41:13.537 --> 00:41:16.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
but half of the
Democrats in Congress

00:41:16.273 --> 00:41:17.575
were saying how this is proof

00:41:17.575 --> 00:41:19.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
that America is a white
supremacist nation

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:21.812
and it turns out that
it was all nonsense.

00:41:21.812 --> 00:41:24.281
- Exactly, including our
now sitting vice-president.

00:41:24.281 --> 00:41:25.115
- Yeah.

00:41:25.115 --> 00:41:26.250
(Dave chuckles)

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:27.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
Speaking of our
sitting vice-president,

00:41:27.451 --> 00:41:29.687
let's talk about the
president for a second.

00:41:29.687 --> 00:41:31.155
I've been saying for a while

00:41:31.155 --> 00:41:33.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the scandal is the
non-scandal around this guy.

00:41:33.958 --> 00:41:35.359
Something is not right with him.

00:41:35.359 --> 00:41:36.961
It seems very obvious.

00:41:36.961 --> 00:41:38.829
Everyone online is
talking about it,

00:41:38.829 --> 00:41:42.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
yet no one in corporate
press is talking about it.

00:41:42.166 --> 00:41:44.401 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like, I dunno,
what do you think?

00:41:44.401 --> 00:41:45.536
(Dave chuckles)

00:41:45.536 --> 00:41:47.238
- You know, I don't know.

00:41:47.238 --> 00:41:52.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, if you've ever known
somebody with dementia,

00:41:53.444 --> 00:41:54.979
one of the things
you always notice

00:41:54.979 --> 00:41:58.582 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that they'll have
this quick moment

00:41:58.582 --> 00:42:00.818
where they forget
where they are,

00:42:00.818 --> 00:42:03.754
they almost get like a sense
of terror across their face,

00:42:03.754 --> 00:42:04.889
and then they recover.

00:42:04.889 --> 00:42:06.724 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is sort of
earlier onset dementia,

00:42:06.724 --> 00:42:08.659
this is not when people
are really advanced.

00:42:08.659 --> 00:42:12.096
And there are some times
like Biden reacts in a way

00:42:12.096 --> 00:42:14.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
where I almost think
he has that look,

00:42:14.298 --> 00:42:17.101
that sort of look where he
forgot where he was a second,

00:42:17.101 --> 00:42:18.736
remembered it very quickly,

00:42:18.736 --> 00:42:21.105
but you watch the guy
on the world stage,

00:42:21.105 --> 00:42:22.606 align:start size:84% position:16%
you watch him in some
of these meetings

00:42:22.606 --> 00:42:26.977
and it's just very clear
that he's lost something,

00:42:26.977 --> 00:42:28.479
he's missed a step.

00:42:28.479 --> 00:42:30.247 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know if that's like,
you know, he's just older

00:42:30.247 --> 00:42:33.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he's aged less
gracefully than other people,

00:42:33.083 --> 00:42:35.252
or if he actually has
a serious problem,

00:42:35.252 --> 00:42:38.022
but I find it bizarre that
people never talk about this.

00:42:38.022 --> 00:42:41.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, you probably remember
a couple of years ago,

00:42:41.025 --> 00:42:44.662
like it was a serious
mainstream story to ask

00:42:44.662 --> 00:42:47.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
whether Donald Trump had
like dementia or Alzheimer's.

00:42:47.698 --> 00:42:50.935
Because he like mangled
a sentence in a speech

00:42:50.935 --> 00:42:52.436
or something like that,

00:42:52.436 --> 00:42:55.172
or he got like, you know, he
said the same thing a few times

00:42:55.172 --> 00:42:56.407
in some remarks.

00:42:56.407 --> 00:42:57.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
- He drank water
like this, remember?

00:42:57.841 --> 00:42:59.143 align:start size:97% position:3%
- He drank water like that.
- Two hands.

00:42:59.143 --> 00:43:00.744
- And it's like the
guy has Alzheimer's,

00:43:00.744 --> 00:43:03.747
and it's like, you know,
if you've ever met Trump,

00:43:03.747 --> 00:43:05.149
like he does not come across

00:43:05.149 --> 00:43:07.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
as a guy who doesn't
know where he is.

00:43:07.117 --> 00:43:10.387
And Biden is just so
much worse on this,

00:43:10.387 --> 00:43:12.790 align:start size:97% position:3%
and yet the press is
totally uninterested in it.

00:43:12.790 --> 00:43:14.592
I find it pretty bizarre.

00:43:14.592 --> 00:43:16.727
The other side of
this that is weird

00:43:16.727 --> 00:43:17.962
is that it makes you wonder,

00:43:17.962 --> 00:43:19.697
like who actually
has control, right?

00:43:19.697 --> 00:43:21.065 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is it the White
House chief of staff?

00:43:21.065 --> 00:43:22.299
Is it the vice president?

00:43:22.299 --> 00:43:23.834
Is it some other staffer,

00:43:23.834 --> 00:43:25.669
but like who's making
the decisions there?

00:43:25.669 --> 00:43:29.139
Like I'd love to actually
see just a good documentary

00:43:29.139 --> 00:43:31.875
or piece of reporting
about what's actually,

00:43:31.875 --> 00:43:34.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
or who is actually in
control in the White House.

00:43:34.011 --> 00:43:37.047
- That's exactly what I asked
Trump about two weeks ago.

00:43:37.047 --> 00:43:38.382
Who do you think is in charge?

00:43:38.382 --> 00:43:40.017
He actually didn't
have a great answer,

00:43:40.017 --> 00:43:42.820
but all the time now we
see Biden get out there,

00:43:42.820 --> 00:43:44.788 align:start size:97% position:3%
he fumbles through
something and then he says,

00:43:44.788 --> 00:43:46.590
"Oh, I'm gonna get in trouble."

00:43:46.590 --> 00:43:49.226
It's like, with who?

00:43:49.226 --> 00:43:50.260
- That's right.

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:51.095
(J.D. laughs)

00:43:51.095 --> 00:43:52.563
That's right.

00:43:52.563 --> 00:43:55.566 align:start size:97% position:3%
- With like, come on, to quote
Joe Biden, "Come on, man."

00:43:55.566 --> 00:43:57.668
Like with who are you
gonna get in trouble?

00:43:57.668 --> 00:43:59.770
But we just don't know.

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:02.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you think Kamala is
taking over at some point?

00:44:02.406 --> 00:44:04.608
- You know, I always assumed,

00:44:04.608 --> 00:44:07.111
you know, I've become
more conspiracy-minded

00:44:07.111 --> 00:44:10.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
just because it
seems like our elites

00:44:10.314 --> 00:44:13.183
really are sometimes malicious,

00:44:13.183 --> 00:44:16.920 align:start position:0%
and my assumption is that Kamala
will eventually take over,

00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:20.024
maybe it's in 2024
as the candidate,

00:44:20.024 --> 00:44:21.458 align:start size:81% position:19%
maybe it's even
earlier than that.

00:44:21.458 --> 00:44:24.461 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, if Joe Biden resigns
in the next two years,

00:44:24.461 --> 00:44:26.530
there is nothing that you will
be able to do to convince me

00:44:26.530 --> 00:44:28.198 align:start size:81% position:19%
this was not a
massive conspiracy

00:44:28.198 --> 00:44:30.834
to install a very
unpopular person,

00:44:30.834 --> 00:44:34.038
but like, you know, I'll save
the speculation until later.

00:44:34.038 --> 00:44:37.408
My sense is that like
the corporate wing

00:44:37.408 --> 00:44:41.845 align:start position:0%
of the Democrat Party recognizes
like Kamala's their gal,

00:44:41.845 --> 00:44:44.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
but also even the voters
of the Democrat Party

00:44:44.081 --> 00:44:47.284 align:start size:97% position:3%
don't like her, and so there's
some sort of weird bargain

00:44:47.284 --> 00:44:49.753 align:start position:0%
going on where she's eventually
gonna assume the mantle

00:44:49.753 --> 00:44:52.690
without real democratic
control over the process,

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:53.957
and you know, of course,

00:44:53.957 --> 00:44:55.726
the country's gonna be
that worse off for it,

00:44:55.726 --> 00:44:57.194
but hopefully, you know,

00:44:57.194 --> 00:44:59.196
on our side and we can
stop that from happening.

00:44:59.196 --> 00:45:01.298
- Right, so to get us
back where we started

00:45:01.298 --> 00:45:04.968
to finish this thing up,
so for the average Democrat

00:45:04.968 --> 00:45:09.073
that gets everything you've
said here, that they get it,

00:45:09.073 --> 00:45:10.441
but they're just afraid,

00:45:10.441 --> 00:45:12.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
afraid of being
called a Republican,

00:45:12.376 --> 00:45:14.712 align:start size:84% position:16%
afraid of being
called a conservative,

00:45:14.712 --> 00:45:16.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
afraid of being called
a Trump supporter,

00:45:16.513 --> 00:45:17.781
you know, all the stuff

00:45:17.781 --> 00:45:19.583
that it sounds like you
got over at some point,

00:45:19.583 --> 00:45:20.784
I got over at some point,

00:45:20.784 --> 00:45:23.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I get that those
are legit fears.

00:45:23.854 --> 00:45:26.190
What's your best pitch
sort of as a candidate,

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:28.859
but also as kind of like a
thought leader in this thing

00:45:28.859 --> 00:45:31.462
